## Decoding the question
A **10-mark note** (about **550–650 words**). The UN conferences have **never been a descriptive question**, though they are an MCQ favourite, and the thirtieth review of Beijing in ==2025== makes them topical. Cover all **four** conferences briefly, **Beijing** in detail, **Beijing+30**, Pakistan and a short assessment.

## The model answer

### 1. Background

#### i) The UN's founding commitments
The **UN Charter** (==1945==) affirmed "equal rights of men and women", and the **Commission on the Status of Women** was set up on ==21 June 1946==. The UN adopted the **Convention on the Political Rights of Women** (==1952==) and the **Declaration on the Elimination of Discrimination against Women** (==1967==).

#### ii) The Year and the Decade
The UN declared ==1975== **International Women's Year** and ==1976–1985== the **UN Decade for Women**, with the themes "**Equality, Development and Peace**". Four World Conferences on Women gave this agenda its milestones.

### 2. The first three conferences

#### i) Mexico City (1975)
The first conference adopted a World Plan of Action and led to UNIFEM and INSTRAW; it exposed tensions between Northern women's focus on equality and Southern women's focus on development and poverty.

#### ii) Copenhagen (1980)
At mid-decade it held a signing ceremony for **CEDAW** (adopted ==1979==) and stressed employment, health and education.

#### iii) Nairobi (1985)
It adopted the **Forward-Looking Strategies** to the year 2000 and hosted a large NGO forum, where the Southern network **DAWN** presented its vision of development led by poor women.

::: fig timeline | The UN World Conferences on Women and the Beijing reviews.
@layout vertical
1975: Mexico City — World Plan of Action
1980: Copenhagen — CEDAW signing ceremony
1985: Nairobi — Forward-Looking Strategies
1995: Beijing — Declaration and Platform for Action
2000–2020: Reviews every five years at the CSW
2025: Beijing+30 — progress, gaps, backlash
:::

### 3. Beijing 1995

#### i) The conference
The Fourth World Conference, held in **Beijing** from ==4 to 15 September 1995== with **189** governments, adopted the **Beijing Declaration and Platform for Action** — still the most comprehensive global agenda for women. About 30,000 activists attended the parallel NGO forum at Huairou, and **Benazir Bhutto** addressed the conference.

#### ii) The 12 critical areas
Poverty, education, health, violence, armed conflict, the economy, power and decision-making, institutional mechanisms, human rights, the media, the environment and the girl child.

#### iii) Its strategy and context
It adopted **gender mainstreaming** as a global strategy and declared that "women's rights are human rights", building on the **Vienna** human-rights conference (==1993==) and the **Cairo** population conference (==1994==); Security Council Resolution **1325** (==2000==) later carried the critical area on armed conflict into peace and security.

### 4. Beijing+30 (2025)

#### i) What the review found
The **Beijing+30** review at the CSW in **March 2025** found real progress in **girls' education** and in **laws** against discrimination and violence, but warned of slow change in **power and pay**, persistent **violence**, and a growing **backlash** against women's rights; no country had achieved full equality.

#### ii) Where Beijing lives now
The commitments now run through **SDG 5** (==2015==), with targets for 2030, and the **Generation Equality Forum** (==2021==) sought fresh pledges from governments and donors.

### 5. Pakistan

#### i) The machinery
The conferences shaped the **Women's Division** (==1979==), the **National Plan of Action** (==1998==) after Beijing, accession to **CEDAW** (==1996==), the **National Commission on the Status of Women** (==2000==), the **National Policy for Development and Empowerment of Women** (==2002==) and the **60 reserved seats** (==2002==), which echo Critical Area G on power and decision-making.

#### ii) A platform for activists
Pakistan reports periodically to the **CEDAW Committee**, NGOs submit **shadow reports**, and the Committee has urged the repeal of discriminatory laws and stronger action against violence and child marriage.

### 6. Assessment

#### i) Achievements
The conferences set **global standards**, put violence against women on the human-rights agenda, created national machineries and gave NGOs a voice; **UN Women** (==2010==), first led by **Michelle Bachelet**, gave the agenda a stronger institutional home.

#### ii) Limits
Their outcomes are **not binding**: implementation depends on governments, reservations weaken commitments, and critics call them "Western".

#### iii) The verdict
Beijing+30 shows both sides — a framework that changed the world's language on women, and a promise still far from kept.

## Why this answer scores
- Covers **all four conferences** with dates and one outcome each, and **Beijing** in detail (189 governments, 12 areas, mainstreaming).
- Reports what the **Beijing+30** review found — the topical hook.
- Links the conferences to **Pakistan's machinery** (1979, 1996, 1998, 2000, 2002).
- Ends with a **balanced assessment**; a **timeline** figure shows the sequence.
